    {
      "paperId": "/chapter/10.1007/978-3-319-06004-0_3",
      "doi": "10.1007/978-3-319-06004-0_3",
      "title": "MRI Technique, Contrast, Safety, Anatomy, and Differential",
      "authors": [
        {
          "name": "Shahid M. Hussain, Michael F. Sorrell"
        }
      ],
      "year": 2015,
      "abstract": "This is the largest part of the book with four major subgroups: 1. Metastases from the colorectal cancer 2. Metastases from the non-colorectal malignancies 3. Primary solid liver lesions in cirrhotic liver 4. Primary solid liver lesions in non-cirrhotic liver A number of examples have been provided to illustrate the typical and atypical colorectal liver metastases. Solid liver lesions are best evaluated as follows: (1) the first step is to assess the heavily T2-weighted single-shot turbo spin echo (SSTSE) to exclude any fluid-filled liver lesions such as cysts or hemangiomas; the metastases are either difficult to detect or completely obscured on the SSTSE; the reason is the magnetic transfer contrast (MTC) effect caused by the multiple refocusing pulses; (2) the second step is to assess the lesions on the moderately T2-weighted sequences (fat-suppressed respiratory-triggered TSE and/or DWI with low b-value of 20); (3) the third step is to assess for any fatty infiltration of the lesion and liver on the chemical shift or Dixon images; and (4) the fourth step is to assess the enhancement pattern on the post-contrast T1-weighted arterial and later phases. After these steps, you should be able to detect and characterize the majority of solid liver lesions. We also include in the overall assessment the diffusion-weighted images (DWI) and apparent diffusion coefficient (ADC) as problem-solving sequences, for instance, in atypical lesions, treated metastases, and recurrent or residual disease after locoregional treatment. The hepatobiliary phase imaging is also used as problem-solving tool, for instance, for (a) the distinction between the primary and secondary liver lesions, (b) distinction between focal nodular hyperplasia and adenomas, and (c) biliary leakage after surgery. We do not routinely apply the liver-specific contrast agents to assess hepatocellular carcinomas in cirrhotic liver. Several new cases have been included that illustrate our overall liver MRI approach for the solid liver lesions. Also the role of the liver-specific contrast media is illustrated by providing comparative examples. Of particular mention are the new items: 1. New examples of a rare hepatic adrenal rest tumor (HART) that presented as a fat-containing liver lesion and a hepatic lipoma demonstrate the importance of Dixon-based imaging to detect the presence of fat within the lesions. 2. Colorectal liver metastasis in a severely fatty infiltrated liver: the appearance of the metastases dramatically changes on the MR imaging sequences. This can be confusing and could potentially lead to misdiagnosis. 3. Role of the liver-specific gadolinium-based contrasts agents (GBCAs) as a problem-solving tool is highlighted by several new cases. 4. New classification of the hepatocellular adenomas has been described. 5. Reporting of (suspected) hepatocellular carcinomas (HCCs) in a cirrhotic liver based on the OPTN and UNOS criteria has been provided in two simple tables. 6. Several new cases of HCC have been included to illustrate the role of the liver-specific GBCAs, DWI, and subtraction images. 7. A new case of a large intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma was included to show the specific tumor anatomy and correlation between DWI and FDG-PET imaging",
      "abstract_not_available": false,
